Dice and cup renders -- packages/dice-ui/assets/dice/*.webp, cup.webp
======================================================================

Unlike packages/mahjong-tile-ui/assets/ and packages/spanish-deck-ui's own
deck art, nothing in this directory is someone else's illustration
rasterized. There is no attribution obligation to carry here, and this file
records exactly why, so that claim stays checkable rather than taken on
faith.

Geometry
  Modeled from primitives in ../tools/render-props.py: a beveled cube with
  boolean-carved pip craters (the six die faces), and a solidified open
  cylinder with a felt-lined interior (the cup). Original work by this
  repository, same as every other shape this codebase draws — no source
  asset, no derivative, nothing for a license to attach to.

Ivory material (the six die faces)
  Fully procedural: a plain Principled BSDF plus a Noise/Bump grain and an
  Ambient-Occlusion-driven crevice tint, all authored as Blender shader
  nodes in ../tools/render-props.py. No photographic input of any kind.

Leather material (cup.webp only)
  Photographic PBR scan: "Brown Leather" by Rob Tuytel, published by Poly
  Haven — https://polyhaven.com/a/brown_leather
  License: CC0 1.0 Universal (public domain dedication)
  https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/

  CC0 asks for nothing: no attribution, no share-alike, no notice
  requirement of any kind. This section exists purely so the source is
  traceable, not because anything here is legally required.

  Fetched at 1k resolution (albedo, roughness, OpenGL-space normal map) by
  `render-props.py fetch`, applied as a UV-mapped material with a small
  colour tint and a shader-only cross-stitch overlay (no separate artwork),
  and baked into the final render. The green felt interior and the ivory
  die material carry no photographic input at all — see above.

Changes made
  Everything here IS the change: this is a from-scratch render, not an
  edit of an existing file. Recorded for completeness rather than because
  any license requires it:
  1. The leather scan was UV-mapped onto original cup geometry, tinted, and
     composited with a procedural cross-stitch seam.
  2. Every other visible surface (ivory, felt) is fully procedural.
  3. All seven images were rendered transparent and re-encoded from PNG to
     lossy WebP (quality 88) — see ../tools/render-props.py's own `encode`
     step.
